# chibi-bytevector: a syntax-rules rule with four elements
#
# `chibi/ieee-754.scm:16`, in `bytes-u8-set-all!`:
#
#     ((_) bv off i)
#
# is a rule of four elements where a rule has two, a pattern and a template.
# Patina rejects it, Gauche rejects it ("malformed macro") and Chez rejects
# it; chibi reads the pattern `(_)` and the template `bv` and ignores the
# rest. It is a parenthesisation slip for the macro's base case - no bytes
# left, nothing to set - which the two rules below it show the shape of:
#
#     ((_ bv off i) #f)
#
# **It changes nothing that runs.** Every use of the macro supplies at least
# one byte, so the recursion ends at the second rule and neither spelling of
# the first is ever reached - which is why chibi has never noticed. Upstream
# still has the slip at `bb9b3215` (2026-09-09), so this cannot be quoted from
# there; it is worth reporting.
#
# **One token, five packages.** A dependency's patch is staged for everything
# that depends on it, so this also unblocks chibi-crypto-md5, -rsa and -sha2,
# which were excluded only "via (chibi bytevector)". The fifth, postgresql,
# gets past it and turns out to need `(foreign c)` - FFI, and re-filed as such.
#
# Measured after, both backends, assertions: chibi-bytevector 69 of 69,
# chibi-crypto-md5 3 of 3, chibi-crypto-rsa 46 of 46, chibi-crypto-sha2 38 of
# 38. None of the four had run an assertion before.
diff -ruN a/chibi/ieee-754.scm b/chibi/ieee-754.scm
--- a/chibi/ieee-754.scm
+++ b/chibi/ieee-754.scm
@@ -14,7 +14,7 @@
 
 (define-syntax bytes-u8-set-all!
   (syntax-rules ()
-    ((_) bv off i)
+    ((_ bv off i) #f)
     ((_ bv off i b1) (bytevector-u8-set! bv (+ off i) b1))
     ((_ bv off i b1 b2 b3 ...)
      (begin
